# Number of exactly eight bytes, or termination. Every attribute of the
# package that reaches a Java atom needs an IEEE-754 double, so a receiver
# of any other width would fail later, at `BytesRaw.whenFit`, naming an
# attribute the caller never wrote. Stated once here, with the message form
# of `u64` and `i64`: width is the culprit, not the attribute that happened
# to read it first (#9893).

+architect yegor256@gmail.com
+home https://github.com/objectionary/eo
+package number
+spdx SPDX-FileCopyrightText: Copyright (c) 2016-2026 Objectionary.com
+spdx SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
+version 0.0.0

[^num] > checked
  if. > @
    num.as-bytes.size.eq 8
    number num.as-bytes
    T
      "A number needs exactly 8 bytes, got %d".printf
        * num.as-bytes.size

  42.checked.eq 42 ++> can-return-a-number-of-eight-bytes

  checked. --> stops-on-a-number-that-is-not-eight-bytes
    number "abc"
